{#
    A section of a document: a heading and everything under it, down to the
    next heading of its level.

    The theme draws this rather than taking what the renderer gives, and the
    class is the system's own — a page whose sections are named `section` is a
    page whose spacing has to be written against somebody else's markup, and
    the one rule that gives a heading its air reaches a *sibling*, which the
    wrapper stands in the way of. Owning the box is what lets the rule state
    the distance between two sections instead of guessing it.

    A `<section>` and not a `<div>`: it is one. Deliberately unnamed — a named
    section is a landmark, and thirty nested landmarks is a page a screen
    reader cannot get out of.

    `:class:` is carried through: dropping what a theme does not understand is
    the one thing it must not do.
#}
<section class="sds-section{% if node.classes %} {{ node.classesString }}{% endif %}" id="{{ node.title.id }}">
    {%- for childNode in node.children -%}
        {{ renderNode(childNode) }}
    {%- endfor -%}
</section>
